<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Classroom News</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> We Need Pictures for the Classroom Newsletter.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: text: Explain the main points of class readings to peers. communication: Assist in resolving misunderstandings among peers.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can assist peers in resolving misunderstandings by rephrasing and using visuals (e.g., writing 'First, write; then, draw,' with matching pictures). Can explain the main points of class readings by summarizing key ideas with visuals (e.g., writing 'school trip' and pointing to a bus and museum drawing). Can support classmates in understanding newsletter themes by writing and showing visuals (e.g., writing 'school events' and pointing to drawings of class activities).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><i>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</i></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows three different pictures on the board or projector: 1. Students working on a class project, 2. A school sports day, 3. A school trip to a museum. The teacher asks: "What do you see in the pictures?" "Which picture shows a fun <b>event</b>?" "What <b>topic</b> is this picture about?" Students respond, recalling vocabulary like 'event,' 'class,' 'school,' 'pictures.' (5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher shows a simple paragraph about a past school event (e.g., "Yesterday, we went on a field trip to the Emberá Community. We learned about their culture. It was very exciting!") and a drawing of a bus and a traditional house. The teacher says, "This is a short <b>article</b> for our <b>newsletter</b>. I will <b>explain</b> it to you simply. 'Yesterday, we went to the Emberá Community.' (points to bus). 'We learned about their culture.' (points to house). This was a fun school <b>event</b>." The teacher models rephrasing and using visuals to summarize. The teacher also models emphasizing the main verb in future tense contractions: "I'll *write* the article." (5 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of mediation with visuals. "Do I use only words to explain, or pictures too?" (Expected: Pictures too). "If someone doesn't understand, what can I do?" (Expected: Rephrase, show a picture, <b>explain</b> again). (3 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(13 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short, simple "Newsletter Announcement" on the board or a handout, introducing the "Class Event Plan" project. The announcement includes roles and responsibilities for creating an article about the upcoming event.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Newsletter Announcement:</b><br>
                <b>Headline:</b> Our Next Big Class Event!<br>
                Dear students,<br>
                We are planning a special Class Art Fair! It will be a fun event where we will show our creative <b>art</b>. We need your help to make a great <b>article</b> for our next <b>newsletter</b> about this <b>event</b>.<br>
                <b>What do we need?</b> We need <b>pictures</b>! And we need students to <b>write</b> about the fair.<br>
                <b>Who will do what?</b><br>
                - Anna: "I will <b>organize</b> the <b>pictures</b>."<br>
                - Ben: "I will <b>write</b> the main <b>paragraph</b> about the art fair."<br>
                - Carla: "I will <b>explain</b> the different types of art."<br>
                When did we decide this? Yesterday, we discussed it in <b>class</b>. What will you do to help? (7 min)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Describe plans for events: 'I will write the article.' Request materials: 'We need pictures for the newsletter.' Narrating past events: 'Yesterday, we went on a field trip.' Sociolinguistic: Collaborate in writing projects, clarifying roles and responsibilities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students answer gist questions orally: "What is the new <b>event</b> about?" (Expected: Class Art Fair). "What do we need for the <b>newsletter</b> <b>article</b>?" (Expected: Pictures and students to write). "Who will <b>organize</b> the <b>pictures</b>?" (Expected: Anna). (3 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(10 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Mediation: explaining main points, assisting in resolving misunderstandings by rephrasing and using visuals. Grammar: Present Simple (questions), Future (affirmative/interrogative), Past Simple (affirmative/interrogative). Vocabulary: <i>article, newsletter, event, pictures, write, explain, organize, share, creative, fun, simple, exciting.</i> Pronunciation: Emphasizing main verb in future tense contractions (e.g., 'I'll *organize*'). Phonemic awareness: identifying /ɑ/ in 'art' and /oʊ/ in 'show'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Visual Matching & Summarizing (5 min)</b>
                        The teacher gives students a short written summary of a past school event (e.g., "Last month, we had a science fair. Students showed their projects. It was very interesting.") and three small drawings: a beaker, a student presenting, a trophy. In pairs, students match each sentence of the summary to a drawing and then orally summarize the event using the drawings. They practice saying, "This drawing shows...", "This means...". Teacher monitors for vocabulary use (<i>event, project, explain</i>) and simple explanations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Rephrasing Roles (5 min)</b>
                        The teacher writes short, simple statements about roles for the "Class Art Fair" (e.g., "Ben is writing the article." "Anna organizing photos."). Students work in pairs. One student reads a statement. The other student must rephrase it using a future tense contraction and a visual clue, like drawing a quick stick figure. Example: "Ben is writing the article." Partner says, "Ben *will write* the <b>article</b>." (draws a pen). Teacher emphasizes the verb 'write'. Students also practice identifying /ɑ/ in 'art' and /oʊ/ in 'show' as they use related words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: Explain an Idea with Visuals (5 min)</b>
                        Students work in new pairs. Each pair receives a small card with a single idea for the "Class Art Fair" (e.g., "We need colorful posters." "Students can show paintings." "Invite parents to the event."). Student A explains the idea to Student B, using only 5 words or less and a simple drawing on a mini-whiteboard or scrap paper. Student B tries to understand and repeats the idea. Then they switch roles. The focus is on using visuals to mediate a simple message and resolving any misunderstanding. Example: Student A: "Colorful posters!" (draws a colorful poster). Student B: "We need <b>colorful posters</b>!"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(15 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group "Class Event Plan" explanation with visual support.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group imagines they are planning a new class event (e.g., "Class Talent Show," "Reading Day," "Recycling Project") for the upcoming classroom newsletter. They must decide on:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The name of the <b>event</b>.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What will happen (2-3 main activities) – using future tense (e.g., "We will sing songs.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Who will do what (<b>roles</b> and <b>responsibilities</b> – using 'I will...' statements and sociolinguistic language for teamwork, e.g., "I will be responsible for the layout." "Can you <b>organize</b> the <b>pictures</b>?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Why they need <b>pictures</b> for the <b>newsletter</b> <b>article</b>.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Each group prepares a simple poster or large sheet of paper with keywords, simple drawings, and short sentences to illustrate their plan. They must include at least two drawings or symbols per activity. Then, each group takes turns presenting their "Class Event Plan" to another group. They must clearly <b>explain</b> the main points of their plan and clarify any misunderstandings using their visuals and rephrasing when necessary. For instance, if a peer asks, "What will you do?" a student might say, "We will *share* our talents!" while pointing to a drawing of a microphone. (8 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(8 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist of mediation skills during group presentations.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group explains their "Class Event Plan" (prepared in Stage 4) to the entire class. The teacher observes how effectively students mediate their plan, using visuals and verbal explanations, and how they assist in resolving any peer misunderstandings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially emphasizing the main verb in future tense contractions (e.g., "I'll *share*," "We'll *show*").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of target vocabulary (<i>event, newsletter, article, pictures, organize, explain, share, creative</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanation of the event plan.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to rephrase or use visuals (drawings, keywords) to aid understanding.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and clear assignment of roles within the group using appropriate sociolinguistic language.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two short sentences: "I can <b>explain</b> our class <b>event</b> plan to a friend by..." and "I used <b>pictures</b> to help my friends understand when I said...". (7 min)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(7 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy about explaining our class event plan using pictures and words?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult? How can I improve my explanations next time?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this mediation lesson? Were students able to effectively use visuals to explain their "Class Event Plan" and resolve misunderstandings?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds are needed for future mediation tasks, especially regarding rephrasing or summarization at A2.1? For example, more practice with sentence frames for explaining.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully integrate the target grammar (present, future, past simple), vocabulary, and sociolinguistic competencies?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the theme by consolidating language and applying mediation skills to a project. The skills practiced here, such as explaining plans and collaborating, will be fundamental for future group projects and communicative tasks in English, laying the groundwork for more complex information exchange and problem-solving in subsequent themes.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">___ / ___ / ___</p>
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(2 min)</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>